head><body>Pradeep, Johanna, Megan,<br><br>Good analysis, Pradeep. I agree =
- quoting this as machined-from-billet in 6061-T6 is the right call. The ca=
sting approach would require secondary machining anyway to hold the GD&amp;=
T callouts, and we'd be dealing with porosity inspection headaches on every=
 lot.<br><br>Johanna - go ahead and prepare the quote based on:<br>- Materi=
al: 6061-T6 aluminum billet (we have reliable supply through Alcoa and pote=
ntially Northwest Metals)<br>- Process: 3-axis CNC machining with secondary=
 deburr and anodize<br>- Quantity breaks: 500, 1000, and 2500 pieces<br>- L=
ead time: 4-6 weeks ARO for production quantities<br><br>Include a note exp=
laining why we're recommending machined vs. cast. Pacific Auto may push bac=
k on price since castings are cheaper per unit, but the quality advantage a=
nd our ability to hold tolerances will be the differentiator.<br><br>I'll h=
ave Pradeep run a quick DFM review and flag any features that might drive c=
ost, so you can address them proactively in the quote.<br><br>Alex<br><br>O=
n 7/22/2026, 10:58:29 PM, &quot;Pradeep Gupta&quot; &lt;PradeepG@854jpt.onm=
icrosoft.com&gt; wrote:<br>&gt; Alex, Johanna,
<br>&gt;=20
<br>&gt; I've reviewed the Pacific Auto TMB-2028 drawings. A few observatio=
ns:
<br>&gt;=20
<br>&gt; 1. Material - A356-T6 is a casting alloy. Given the GD&amp;T requi=
rements on this part, I'd strongly recommend we quote it as a machined-from=
-billet part in 6061-T6 instead. The true position callout of 0.005&quot; o=
n the mounting holes is extremely tight for a casting. Machined from billet=
, it's very achievable for us.
<br>&gt;=20
<br>&gt; 2. The overall geometry is straightforward - I can program this fo=
r our Haas VF-4 in 2 setups. Cycle time estimate: approximately 18 minutes/=
part.
<br>&gt;=20
<br>&gt; 3. One concern: the drawing references a proprietary surface treat=
ment spec (PA-ST-2028-Rev.A) that I don't have. Johanna, can you request th=
is from Pacific Auto?
<br>&gt;=20
<br>&gt; 4. If we quote machined instead of cast, our price point will be h=
igher but the quality will be significantly better. I think we should posit=
ion this as a value-add.
<br>&gt;=20
<br>&gt; Rough cost estimate: $38-44/unit at 8,000/year volume in 6061-T6 m=
achined. I can have a detailed quote breakdown ready by Monday.
